  The four re-authentications, read from the frame times above:

  | re-auth | EAP-Request/Identity at | gap from the one before |
  | ------- | ----------------------- | ----------------------- |
  | #1      | 30.049 s                | 30.049 s                |
  | #2      | 60.093 s                | 30.044 s                |
  | #3      | 90.141 s                | 30.048 s                |
  | #4      | 120.190 s               | 30.049 s                |

  => STEP 1 PASS: the DUT re-authenticated the supplicant four times.  Every gap is
     within 49 milliseconds of the configured 30 seconds.  Three further things are
     proved by the same frame list.  First, the DUT starts each cycle: every cycle opens
     with an EAP-Request/Identity from the DUT, and the supplicant sent no EAPOL-Start
     after t=0.003 s.  The timer belongs to the DUT.  Second, each cycle is a full EAP
     exchange, not a keepalive: the challenge is different every time (four different
     digests) and the EAP id pair is new every time (3/4, 5/6, 7/8, 9/10), so the RADIUS
     server is asked again on every cycle.  Third, the server round trip is 1.00 s on
     every cycle, measured from the MD5 response to the EAP-Success.

